# core.autocrlf is true in this clone, so a fresh checkout would rewrite these
# files with CRLF. Both are GENERATED by sync_phase_module.py from canonical
# modules that live outside this repo and are written with LF. The sync's
# --check mode byte-compares the generated copies against canonical, so a CRLF
# checkout would make it report drift that does not exist.
#
# Pin the generated files to LF so the comparison stays meaningful.
src/reports.js text eol=lf
src/phase1.js  text eol=lf

# 2026-07-31: App.jsx and data.js were NOT pinned, and a deploy warned
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it". They are not
# generated wholesale, but they are machine-edited:
#   - gen_workbook_programs.py splices programs 26-36 into data.js
#   - rebalance_legs_p1_p12.py --check reads data.js
#   - test_workbook_programs_pwa.mjs / test_pwa_parity_engine.mjs compare them
#     against fitness_app.html
# Those writers emit LF. If a checkout, merge or clone hands them back as CRLF,
# the next scripted edit produces a whole-file diff and the parity suites start
# failing in a way that looks like a real regression. That exact hazard is
# recorded in CLAUDE.md; this is the other half of the fix.
#
# The project convention is LF. Pin every source file, not just the generated
# two, so no future file inherits the problem.
*.js   text eol=lf
*.jsx  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf

# EXCEPT batch files. cmd.exe can mis-parse an LF-only .bat - `goto` to a label
# is the classic failure, and deploy.bat uses one. These stay CRLF everywhere.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
